<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Limmat</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Limmat is a river in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> that flows out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-zurich">Lake Zürich</a> through the city of Zürich and runs north-west for about 35 kilometres to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aare">Aare</a>. The old town of Zürich lies on both banks. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> the Anabaptist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/felix-manz">Felix Manz</a> was executed by drowning in the river in 1527.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/limmat.jpg" alt="Limmat" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chris Brown · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Limmat_River.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
